<div class="min-h-screen bg-base-200/40">
  <header class="border-b border-base-300 bg-base-100">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 h-16 flex items-center gap-6">
      <a href={~p"/"} class="flex items-center gap-2 font-semibold">
        <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-primary inline-block" /> SeriouslySimpleAnalytics
      </a>
      <nav class="hidden md:flex items-center gap-5 text-sm text-base-content/70">
        <a href="#ping" class="hover:text-primary">The API</a>
        <a href="#events" class="hover:text-primary">What to log</a>
        <a href="#web" class="hover:text-primary">Websites</a>
        <a href={~p"/llms.txt"} class="hover:text-primary font-mono text-xs">llms.txt</a>
      </nav>
      <span class="flex-1" />
      <.link navigate={~p"/dashboard"} class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Dashboard</.link>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- hero -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 pt-16 pb-10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <div class="badge badge-primary badge-outline mb-4">Free · AI tool analytics</div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-tight">
        Analytics for the AI tool you're building. One URL.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-base-content/70 leading-relaxed">
        You shipped an agent, an MCP server, a CLI, a VS Code extension. You have no idea
        whether anyone runs it twice. Fetch one URL with your account ID and you have usage
        analytics — no SDK to install, no API key to exchange, no JSON to assemble, nothing to
        add to your dependency tree.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 rounded-box bg-base-100 border border-base-300 p-5 sm:p-6">
      <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-base-content/60 mb-3">
        This is the entire integration
      </div>
      <pre class="bg-base-200 rounded p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>{ping_url(@base_url, @site_key)}</code></pre>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <.link navigate={~p"/dashboard"} class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">See it in the dashboard</.link>
        <a href={~p"/llms.txt"} class="btn btn-outline btn-sm font-mono text-xs">
          Read llms.txt
        </a>
        <span class="text-sm text-base-content/50">
          Returns 204. Fire and forget.
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- why -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 pb-16">
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="rounded-box bg-base-100 border border-base-300 p-5">
        <div class="text-sm font-medium">No dependency, in any language</div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
          If it can make an HTTP request it can report. Python, Go, Rust, bash, a Lambda, a
          GitHub Action, a shell one-liner. Nothing to install and nothing to keep updated.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-box bg-base-100 border border-base-300 p-5">
        <div class="text-sm font-medium">Your agent can wire it up itself</div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
          The complete contract is at <a href={~p"/llms.txt"} class="link font-mono">/llms.txt</a>
          — parameters, event conventions, rate guidance, and the rules about what must never be
          sent. Point a coding agent at that URL and it can integrate unattended.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-box bg-base-100 border border-base-300 p-5">
        <div class="text-sm font-medium">Many projects, one account</div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
          Pass <code class="font-mono text-xs">project=</code>
          and each tool you ship reports separately under the same account ID. No new key, no new
          setup.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- the API -->
  <section id="ping" class="border-y border-base-300 bg-base-100">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Every parameter there is</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70 max-w-2xl">
        Four you will use, a few you might, and anything else you add is kept as an attribute on
        the event. There is no schema to extend.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table table-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Parameter</th>
              <th>Meaning</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr :for={
              row <- [
                {"uid", "Your account ID."},
                {"c · cc · s_p · n",
                 "Your user's city, county, state/province and nation. Required — see below."},
                {"event",
                 "What happened — page_view, tool_called, run_completed. Anything you like."},
                {"project",
                 "Which of your tools this is. Keeps them separate under one account."},
                {"type", "Channel. ai for an AI tool; that is the default here."},
                {"sid",
                 "Session ID. Pings that share one are grouped into a run or conversation."},
                {"path", "A screen or step. With event=page_view it becomes a real pageview."},
                {"…anything", "Kept as an attribute: &tool=search&latency_ms=420&outcome=success"}
              ]
            }>
              <td class="font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{elem(row, 0)}</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-base-content/70">{elem(row, 1)}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div>
          <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-base-content/60 mb-2">
            Instrumenting one run
          </div>
          <pre class="bg-base-200 rounded p-4 text-xs overflow-x-auto"><code>{run_example(@base_url, @site_key)}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">
              Sessions and flow, for free
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              Consecutive pings are grouped into a session automatically, and the server numbers
              your pageviews and links each to the one before it. Send
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">event=page_view&amp;path=/chat</code>
              each time — you never send a sequence number — and you get a flow graph showing
              which paths lead where and where runs stop. Pass
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">sid</code>
              when you want exact control.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">event=page_view</code> is special
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              With a <code class="font-mono text-xs">path</code>, it is recorded as a real
              pageview rather than a generic event — so it lands in the pages report and the
              page-to-page flow graph.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">You send the location; we don't guess it</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">c</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs">cc</code>,
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">s_p</code>
              and <code class="font-mono text-xs">n</code>
              carry your user's city, county, state/province and nation. A ping arrives from
              wherever your tool runs — a container, a serverless region three countries away —
              so its address says where the software is, not where the person is. Guessing from
              it would give a confident, wrong answer.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">Your account ID is the whole credential</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              It identifies an account and grants nothing else. Unknown IDs get the same empty 204
              as real ones, so the endpoint cannot be used to test whether an account exists.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- what to log -->
  <section id="events" class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What's worth logging</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70 max-w-2xl">
      Event names are yours to choose. These are the ones that answer the questions you
      actually have about a tool you just shipped.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div
        :for={
          item <- [
            {"install · first_run · upgraded",
             "Is anyone adopting it, and are they staying current? Pass version=."},
            {"run_started · run_completed",
             "How many runs finish. Pass outcome=success|error|cancelled."},
            {"tool_called", "Which capabilities actually get used. Pass tool= and latency_ms=."},
            {"page_view", "Which screens or steps people reach, with path=."},
            {"error", "What breaks in the wild. Pass kind= — never the message text."},
            {"feedback", "Whether it helped. Pass rating=up|down."}
          ]
        }
        class="rounded-box bg-base-100 border border-base-300 p-5"
      >
        <div class="text-sm font-mono">{elem(item, 0)}</div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">{elem(item, 1)}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 rounded-box border border-warning/40 bg-warning/5 p-5">
      <div class="text-sm font-medium">Never send credentials</div>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/70">
        API keys, tokens, passwords, session secrets. Parameters travel in a URL, so by the time
        one reaches us it has already been written into the logs of every proxy it passed
        through. Keep prompts and completions out too — URLs have length limits, and analytics is
        for counting, not transcript storage. The rules are stated in full in <a
          href={~p"/llms.txt"}
          class="link font-mono"
        >/llms.txt</a>, so an agent integrating on
        your behalf reads them too.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- websites -->
  <section id="web" class="border-y border-base-300 bg-base-100">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Got a website too?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70 max-w-2xl">
        The same account covers it. One script tag captures everything below automatically, with
        no tagging — and it reports into the same dashboard as your tools.
      </p>

      <pre class="mt-6 bg-base-200 rounded p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>{script_tag(@base_url, @site_key)}</code></pre>

      <div class="mt-8 grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4">
        <div
          :for={
            item <- [
              {"Dwell vs. engaged time",
               "Counted separately. A tab left open all afternoon is hours of dwell and almost no engagement."},
              {"Max scroll depth", "How far down each page a visitor actually got."},
              {"Page-to-page flow",
               "By URL or title. Click any page to re-centre the diagram and follow a route."},
              {"Outbound clicks", "Caught on mousedown, before the browser tears the page down."},
              {"Forms, field by field",
               "On submit and on abandonment. Passwords are masked in the browser and never sent."},
              {"City, state, country",
               "Resolved at ingest; the address is used for the lookup and then discarded."},
              {"AI crawlers, named",
               "GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot and the rest — filtered out of your human numbers and reported separately."},
              {"Junk filtered, not hidden",
               "Impossible dwell times are held back by default, with the reason shown and a toggle to see them."}
            ]
          }
          class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5"
        >
          <div class="text-sm font-medium">{elem(item, 0)}</div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">{elem(item, 1)}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- privacy -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Privacy by construction</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-x-10 gap-y-4 max-w-4xl">
      <div :for={
        item <- [
          {"No cookies",
           "Identifiers live in browser storage, so there is no consent banner to show."},
          {"No IP addresses stored",
           "Used in-request to resolve a city, then salted, hashed and discarded. The hash rotates daily."},
          {"Credentials are never accepted",
           "Password-typed fields are masked in the browser and dropped again server-side, so a stale snippet cannot defeat it."},
          {"You choose what to send",
           "The ping API records the dimensions you pass and nothing more. Nothing is inferred from a parameter you did not send."},
          {"Self-hosted",
           "Run it yourself and the data stays in your own Postgres. No third party in the path."},
          {"Opt out per element",
           "data-wa-ignore on any element, form or field excludes it entirely."}
        ]
      }>
        <div class="text-sm font-medium">{elem(item, 0)}</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mb-2">{elem(item, 1)}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- cta -->
  <section class="border-t border-base-300 bg-base-100">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Send your first event now</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70">
        Paste the URL at the top into a terminal, then open the dashboard. It will be there.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3 justify-center">
        <.link navigate={~p"/dashboard"} class="btn btn-primary">Open the dashboard</.link>
        <.link href={~p"/demo"} class="btn btn-outline">See the browser tracker</.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer class="border-t border-base-300">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 text-sm">
      <span class="text-base-content/60">SeriouslySimpleAnalytics</span>
      <span class="text-base-content/30">·</span>
      <.link navigate={~p"/dashboard"} class="link link-hover">Dashboard</.link>
      <a href={~p"/llms.txt"} class="link link-hover font-mono text-xs">llms.txt</a>
      <span class="flex-1" />
      <a
        href="mailto:me@LoganBesecker.com?subject=Business%20development%20inquiry"
        class="link link-hover font-medium"
      >
        Business development inquiries
      </a>
    </div>
  </footer>
</div>
